The function, whole (TypeScript, no imports; `src/lib/threads-length.ts`, the copy that stays: the adapter's own copy is deleted and imports this one):

export const THREADS_LIMIT = 500;
const BY_BYTES =
  /[\p{Extended_Pictographic}\p{Emoji_Modifier}\p{Regional_Indicator}‍️⃣]/u;
const utf8Bytes = (cp: number) => (cp < 0x80 ? 1 : cp < 0x800 ? 2 : cp < 0x10000 ? 3 : 4);
function units(ch: string): number {
  return BY_BYTES.test(ch) ? utf8Bytes(ch.codePointAt(0) as number) : 1;
}
export function threadsLength(text: string): number {
  let n = 0;
  for (const ch of text.normalize('NFC')) n += units(ch);
  return n;
}
export function fitsThreads(text: string, reserve = 0): boolean {
  return threadsLength(text) + reserve <= THREADS_LIMIT;
}

Rule R1. Read this first: R1 is OUR READING of the Threads documentation ("500 characters, emoji counted by UTF-8 bytes"). It has not been checked against the live API by anyone here. So there are two different findings, and this task is about the first:
(a) "the function violates R1" = a counterexample, checked by a script in one board check;
(b) "Threads itself counts differently from R1" = valuable, write it as a note with your source; it can change R1, but it is not a counterexample to the function.

R1 in exact words, for every JavaScript string up to 100,000 UTF-16 units:
1. The text is normalized to Unicode NFC first.
2. It is then walked by Unicode code points (a surrogate pair is one code point; a lone surrogate is one code point).
3. A code point counts as the number of bytes of its UTF-8 encoding (2, 3 or 4) when it is any of: a code point with the Unicode property Extended_Pictographic; an emoji modifier (the five skin tones U+1F3FB..U+1F3FF, property Emoji_Modifier); a regional indicator (U+1F1E6..U+1F1FF, the halves of flags); U+200D zero width joiner; U+FE0F variation selector 16; U+20E3 combining enclosing keycap.
4. Every other code point counts 1: letters of every alphabet, digits (also the digit inside a keycap), punctuation, spaces, tabs, line breaks, combining marks that remain after NFC, the tag characters of subdivision flags, a lone surrogate.
5. The length of a text is the sum. Examples by R1: "Привет" 6; "ok 👍" 7; "👍🏽" 8; "👨‍👩‍👧" 18; "🇷🇺" 8; "1️⃣" 7 (1+3+3); "©" 2; "™ и ®" 8; "e"+U+0301 1; "a\nb" 3.
6. fitsThreads(text, reserve) is true exactly when threadsLength(text) + reserve <= 500, for reserve a whole number from 0 to 500 (the reserve is for lines other code adds; today it is 0 and the signature line is part of the text that is counted).
7. The functions never throw and finish in under one second for 100,000 units.

What counts as an accepted counterexample. One string whose length by R1 differs from threadsLength, or a (text, reserve) pair where fitsThreads is wrong by point 6, or a throw. Post it as JSON, inline in your reply:

{"task":"J-P2","input":"<the string, JSON-escaped; \uXXXX for anything invisible or for lone surrogates>","expected":<the length by R1>,"why":"<which point of R1, one line>"}
or {"task":"J-P2","input":"...","reserve":<n>,"expectedFits":true|false,"why":"..."}
For a long input: {"repeat":"<piece>","times":<n>} instead of "input".

What is NOT a counterexample:
- Finding (b) above: "Threads counts it differently". Write it as a note.
- A code point whose Extended_Pictographic property differs between Unicode versions: the check runs on Node 22 or later with its own Unicode tables; name the version difference as a note.
- A reserve outside 0..500, not a whole number, or not a number.
- "The rule should count grapheme clusters / UTF-16 units / bytes for everything." That is a disagreement with R1: say it in a separate line with your reason and source; if it changes R1, your name goes on the change.
- Cutting a long text into parts: another function, another task.

How it is checked, within one board check. The studio saves your JSON and runs:
node jp2-check.mjs your.json
The script computes the R1 value with an independent reference (a different implementation: code point loop, one test per class, TextEncoder for the bytes), runs the function above unchanged, and prints one of: ACCEPTED (your `expected` equals the reference and the function differs), REJECTED function agrees with R1, REJECTED your expected differs from R1 (it prints the reference's value and its per-code-point breakdown, so you can argue with it: a wrong reference is also a finding).

What an accepted counterexample gets. It is fixed in public in the thread, the fix names you in the file header and in CONTRIBUTORS.md, and your string becomes a permanent regression test under your name. A note that changes R1 after the first live calls is credited the same way.

